snapctrlalt (1.4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The toolbar can now be attached to any of the four sides: the small
    triangles in its corners move it to the top/bottom (horizontal) or the
    left/right (vertical, the whole bar is transposed).  The choice is
    remembered in the config; "auto" (follow the selection) is still default.
  * Dragging the toolbar by its empty area slides it along the edge it is
    attached to, always clamped inside the screen -- the whiteboard-style
    behaviour asked for.
  * The editor window (layout 3) uses a Gtk.Grid now: same four sides, with
    the action buttons moving to the opposite edge.
  * Two real bugs fixed on the way: left<->right switching reused stale bar
    dimensions (1204x152 while claiming to be vertical), and the corner
    buttons were large enough to cover the first/last toolbar buttons.

 -- dsh (deepseek harness) <dsh@localhost>  Tue, 29 Sep 2026 08:10:00 +0800

snapctrlalt (1.3.12) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The toolbar of both layouts (canvas and editor) now has a one-click
    "switch layout" button.  It only applies to the current run -- the saved
    default is untouched -- and reopens the overlay on the same frozen frame
    instead of taking a new screenshot.
  * New tools/restart_resident.py: a running resident keeps the old code in
    memory after an upgrade, which is why the UI could still look stale after
    installing a new deb.  The tool compares process start time with the code
    on disk, restarts when needed and reports a stale installation.
  * New tools/verify_live_ui.py: end-to-end checks against the running
    instance instead of a freshly started one.

 -- dsh (deepseek harness) <dsh@localhost>  Tue, 29 Sep 2026 06:40:00 +0800

snapctrlalt (1.3.11) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The colour picker is now part of the toolbar: RGB sliders, a grey-level
    slider, twelve preset swatches, an eyedropper and a live preview.  The
    panel is exactly as wide as the toolbar (never wider), flips above it when
    there is no room below, and is always clamped inside the screen.
  * New setting "click outside the selection restarts it" (off by default):
    the old QQ behaviour kept clearing a finished selection while annotating.
  * Overlay hit-testing now uses the full toolbar rectangle including the
    colour panel, so clicks on the panel no longer fall through to the canvas.

 -- dsh (deepseek harness) <dsh@localhost>  Tue, 29 Sep 2026 05:30:00 +0800

snapctrlalt (1.3.10) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixed the colour palette being drawn off screen.  Its size was scaled by
    ui_scale and then added to the toolbar position, which is already in canvas
    pixels, while the painter divided by ui_scale again -- so the panel's size
    and position were doubled and the on-screen clamp stopped working.
    Position and size are now computed in canvas pixels throughout, and the
    panel falls back to above the toolbar when there is no room below.

 -- dsh (deepseek harness) <dsh@localhost>  Tue, 29 Sep 2026 04:00:00 +0800

snapctrlalt (1.3.9) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The overlay presentation now uses an in-canvas colour palette instead of a
    Gtk.ColorChooserDialog.  A dialog parented to the fullscreen overlay kept
    ending up underneath it (invisible and unclickable) and the modal loop could
    leave the overlay stuck until Escape was pressed; a plain window, by
    contrast, is unaffected.  The palette is drawn by the overlay itself -- 60
    swatches plus a greyscale row, hover highlight and a live value readout --
    so it is always on top, never steals focus, and never interrupts a capture.
  * The editor presentation keeps using the system dialog, which works there.

 -- dsh (deepseek harness) <dsh@localhost>  Tue, 29 Sep 2026 03:00:00 +0800

snapctrlalt (1.3.8) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Choosing a colour no longer throws the capture away.  While the colour
    dialog was open the overlay kept handling mouse events, so clicks made in
    the dialog were treated as "clicked outside the selection" and cancelled
    the session -- picking a second colour meant capturing again.  The overlay
    now freezes all input while a modal child window is open and restores focus
    afterwards.
  * The colour dialog now opens on a plain left click (it used a non-blocking
    show() whose focus and response were unreliable) and is transient for the
    overlay so it cannot end up behind it.

