snapctrlalt (1.3.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New editor window mode: after selecting a region the capture opens in an
    ordinary window (image in the middle, toolbar above, title bar and close
    button), which the window manager treats like any other window.  Reachable
    with Ctrl+E from the overlay or via toolbar_mode: editor.
  * Annotation drawing is now shared between the overlay and the editor through
    an extracted AnnotationRenderer mixin, so the two presentations cannot
    drift apart.
  * The editor toolbar scales itself down to the available width, so a narrow
    window or a small capture no longer clips its ends.
  * Note about the "floating toolbar over the mask" variant: two attempts
    (a decorated window pinned to the mask, and a small always-on-top window)
    both failed for the same reason -- a separate window above the fullscreen
    overlay never receives mouse clicks, because a background application
    cannot raise its own window over the active one.  Only the canvas and
    editor presentations are offered as a result.

 -- dsh (deepseek harness) <dsh@localhost>  Mon, 28 Sep 2026 20:30:00 +0800

snapctrlalt (1.3.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The annotation toolbar vanished for anyone who had an external screenshot
    tool installed.  "prefer external tool" defaulted to on, so pressing the
    hotkey handed the capture to Flameshot/Spectacle and the user saw that
    program's window instead of this overlay.  The default is now off; switch
    it on from the tray menu or the settings window if you want it.
  * The toolbar is now laid out in two rows (tools on the first, colours,
    widths and actions on the second).  A single row of 29 buttons was 922 px
    wide, which pushed it to the screen edge for narrow selections.
  * Fixed the toolbar being drawn outside the clip region and disappearing
    entirely.  GTK pre-scales the drawing context on HiDPI (a 2880 px canvas
    comes with a 1440 px clip), the overlay then applied ui_scale on top of
    that, so every toolbar coordinate landed past the clip and the whole bar
    was discarded.  The draw handler now resets the transform and ui_scale is
    applied exactly once, while laying out.
  * The layout no longer multiplies the UI scale into its design constants on
    every call, which had been doubling the bar on each frame.

 -- dsh (deepseek harness) <dsh@localhost>  Mon, 28 Sep 2026 19:10:00 +0800

snapctrlalt (1.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixed the annotation toolbar running off the screen for narrow or tall
    selections, which made its leftmost and rightmost buttons unreachable and
    turned clicks near them into a fresh selection.  Two causes:
    the layout mixed two coordinate systems (it placed buttons using canvas
    coordinates but clamped them against UI-unit bounds), and it clamped the
    unscaled size while drawing scales the whole bar by ui_scale -- so a bar
    laid out 922 px wide was drawn 1844 px wide and ended up outside the
    screen.  Placement and clamping now work on the final on-screen rectangle.
  * The vertical placement is clamped into the visible area in every case;
    previously a selection glued to the bottom edge pushed the bar off screen.
  * UI scale detection no longer asks GDK: with GDK_SCALE=1 (which the launcher
    sets so that the coordinate chain stays in device pixels) GDK reports a
    scale factor of 1 for every monitor, so HiDPI screens were misdetected and
    the interface shrank.  It now derives the scale from the panel's physical
    size in millimetres (RandR), the same signal Flameshot uses; SNAP_UI_SCALE
    overrides it.
  * Added a construction smoke test and a toolbar reachability test covering
    nine selection shapes; the latter found the bottom-edge case.

